\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If finitely many cylinders $B_i+R$ $u_i$ cover a convex body $K\subset R^{3}$, with unit axes and measurable finite-area perpendicular bases, must $\Sigma _i$ $area(B_i)/area(proj_{u_i\perp }K)\geq \tfrac12 $? No symmetry assumption is imposed and costs have multiplicity.

\subsection{Short English statement}
If finitely many cylinders $B_i+R$ $u_i$ cover a convex body $K\subset R^{3}$, with unit axes and measurable finite-area perpendicular bases, must $\Sigma _i$ $area(B_i)/area(proj_{u_i\perp }K)\geq \tfrac12 $? No symmetry assumption is imposed and costs have multiplicity.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/README.md}.
