\section*{Directionwise normalized cylinder-covering bound}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that a regular tetrahedron has a finite covering by triangular-base cylinders whose total perpendicular area is strictly less than half its minimum projection area; In manuscript 2 (family 100; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that radial segment sweeps have finite triangular-base cylinder realizations whose area converges to the weighted parameter cost, including a tetrahedral cover below the half-area bound; In manuscript 3 (family 100; 2026-09-27), the asserted result is that varying the axes of the standard two-cylinder tetrahedral cover yields a finite cover below the half-area threshold, also violating the directionwise normalized bound.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), angularly tilted radial families receive a cubic buffer that preserves coverage. Secant-sector calculations give a negative quadratic area correction, and finite triangular enclosures retain the strict saving when the angular mesh is small.

For manuscript 2 (family 100; 2026-09-27), one triangular enclosure is assigned to each interval of a tagged partition. Complementary shortened beams leave a strip covered by horizontal cylinders; exact overlap and strip calculations cancel the first-order cost and leave a strict second-order saving.

For manuscript 3 (family 100; 2026-09-27), tangential angular and rational slope fields preserve the relevant wedges. Complementary label displacements have a controlled second-order overlap surplus, so a small padding secures coverage while tilt lowers perpendicular area by a larger amount.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting results are manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), manuscript 2 (family 100; 2026-09-27), manuscript 3 (family 100; 2026-09-27).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 100; 2026-09-27), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a regular tetrahedron has a finite covering by triangular-base cylinders whose total perpendicular area is strictly less than half its minimum projection area.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

For manuscript 2 (family 100; 2026-09-27), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: radial segment sweeps have finite triangular-base cylinder realizations whose area converges to the weighted parameter cost, including a tetrahedral cover below the half-area bound.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

For manuscript 3 (family 100; 2026-09-27), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: varying the axes of the standard two-cylinder tetrahedral cover yields a finite cover below the half-area threshold, also violating the directionwise normalized bound.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CylinderCovering.lean}, \texttt{RuledCovering.lean}, \texttt{TriangularCovering.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CylinderCovering.fourPaperMain}, \texttt{OAI.RuledApproximation.fullMain}, \texttt{OAI.TriangularCovering.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

